Don Becker (becker@acsu.buffalo.edu) writes:

>Let me ask you a question. If you were playing poker and got a crappy hand,
>you wouldn't consider it to be cheating to stop the game and ask for a redeal
>until you got a royal flush, would you?

Yes, that's cheating.  What isn't cheating, however, is betting a small amount
on the hands where you receive nothing and betting a large amount on the hands
you are dealt a royal flush.

If you think of time as money, he is betting a small amount of time when
he is dealt nothing, but larger amounts when he gets a good deal.

Time is a substantial aspect of nethack to me, and I play the odds with time
every chance I get.  When I have a sucky character, I take risks because it's
less likely for me to win anyway.  If the risks pay off, I've saved time and
if they fail miserably, then I just start over.  But when I've got a good
character, I plan my every move and avoid any avoidable risks.

Actually, I think your poker analogy is good wrt playing nethack.  Nethack
is random as is poker, and I bet time in nethack as I would bet money in
poker.  His decision to restart because certain items were not generated in
his inventory seems a little excessive, but it's really no different than
folding in a poker game.
